# verdict-lint.allowlist. Read by tools/lint-verdict-language.py (INVARIANTS.md §1).
#
# One entry per line: path | context | reason. The entry allows blacklisted words
# on lines of that file that contain the context. The reason says why the word is
# not a verdict there. An entry that no longer matches any line fails the lint as
# stale, so this file cannot drift. The frame sentence "not evidence of wrongdoing"
# needs no entry; it is allowed by the lint itself as Vow II in its own words.
#
# The doctrine quotes the blacklist in order to forbid it.
CHARTER.md | The words we do not use | the Charter defines the blacklist
INVARIANTS.md | Blacklist (v0) | the Invariant defines the blacklist
METHODOLOGY.md | It never says | quotes the list to forbid it
PIPELINE.md | It does not use | quotes the list to forbid it
README.md | It does not say | quotes the list to forbid it
CLAUDE.md | it does not use the words | the working stance quotes the list to forbid it
#
# The law's own categories, describing statutes and not persons.
README.md | Criminal statutes bearing on | a category of statute (18 U.S.C. Ch. 11), not a person
STANDARDS.md | may constitute a criminal violation | the statute's own penalty category
STANDARDS.md | conflict-of-interest criminal statute | the statute's own category
#
# Other senses, and rules about contributors rather than officeholders.
ECOSYSTEM.md | A republisher who prints | an example of the misuse the consumer contract forbids
EVIDENCE.md | Our organisation compromised | the data-integrity sense of the word, about our own capture
CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md | Political disagreement is not misconduct | community conduct of contributors, not an officeholder
CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md | Personal attack is misconduct | community conduct of contributors, not an officeholder
#
# Neighbours' own names, quoted in the related-work section.
RELATED.md | GovTrack names its | a neighbour's own name for its database, quoted
RELATED.md | Federal Contractor Misconduct Database | a neighbour's own database name
RELATED.md | Organized Crime and Corruption Reporting Project | a neighbour's own name
